<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karthaia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Carthaea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karthaia was one of the four ancient cities of the island of Keos in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a>, on its south-east coast. Its ruins include temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and city walls. With Ioulis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koressia">Koressia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poiessa">Poiessa</a> it formed the Keian tetrapolis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karthaia.jpg" alt="Karthaia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phso2 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karthaia_upper_plateau.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The city stood on the Aspri Vigla hills, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> called a narrow ridge of land, between the valleys of the Kalamitsis to the east and the Vathypotamos to the west, with the sea to the south. It was a significant harbour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a>, and a submerged jetty about 160 m long and 35 m wide can still be traced between its two bays. Its territory covered the south-eastern part of Keos, where remains of settlements, farm sheds, towers and metalworking have been found, linked by ancient roads.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site was inhabited for about 1,300 years, from around the 8th century BCE to the 6th century CE, and was then abandoned. Walls more than 2 kilometers long, built between the 6th and 4th centuries BCE, enclosed the acropolis. On its lower terrace stood the temple of Apollo Pythios, of about 530 BCE, the city's most important sanctuary according to ancient texts, where statues were dedicated and the city's decrees displayed. A smaller temple of about 500 BCE on the upper terrace, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> according to some scholars, bore marble sculptures of an Amazonomachy.</p><p>A marble Propylon of the mid-5th century BCE marked the entrance to the upper terrace, and a stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the 4th century BCE, seating almost 1,000 people, lay at the foot of the slope, beside a complex of Roman baths. In the 6th and 7th centuries CE, after the monuments had collapsed, the last inhabitants dug graves among the ruins of the temples, often built of reused architectural pieces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Travellers took the ruins for Ioulis until 1811, when the Danish archaeologist P.O. Brøndsted excavated for two or three weeks and identified the site from inscriptions naming the city; scientific research began soon after 1900 with the Belgian archaeologist P. Graindor. The Greek Ministry of Culture excavated and restored the temples, the Propylon, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and other buildings in 2002–2008 and 2011–2015 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> funding. The site can still be reached only on foot, along cobbled paths, or by small boat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carthaea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early Iron Age Ionian Greeks settled it, and the island was organised as a tetrapolis of four cities, Ioulis, <b>Karthaia</b>, Koressia and Poiessa, which sometimes acted together as one state.</blockquote><a href="/state/keos">Keos</a></li></ul></section>
